  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to show a chance for rain Wednesday as a \nshort wave trough moves from the Great Plains into the Mid \nMississippi Valley.  The GFS shows a couple of QPF bullseyes \nbetween 1.5 and 1.75 inches of rain Wednesday afternoon, but the \nECMWF is much more conservative.  A lot of afternoon convection \ncould keep heat index values well below advisory/warning criteria, \nhowever forecast soundings for Wednesday afternoon show a \nsubstantial low-level capping inversion in the pre-storm \nenvironment.  I'm therefore skeptical about convective initiation. \nTherefore think the heat headlines are still good for Wednesday...and \ncould in fact be needed into the end of the week.\n\nThe latest medium range guidance is now shifting the western upper \nlevel ridge east into the Mississippi Valley Thursday into Friday. \nThis is a change from yesterday's runs where the ridge retreated to \nthe west and a long wave trough dug into the Great Lakes and \nsoutheastern U.S.  The change means that the back door cold front \nwhich was supposed to move through the area in the Thursday/Friday \ntime frame only makes it to central/northern Illinois.  This would \nhave profound impacts on the temperature forecast for the rest of \nthe week into the weekend, and the rainfall forecast for the period \nas well.  The latest NBM still shows a significant temperature drop \non Thursday from the low to mid 90s on Wednesday to the low to mid \n80s for most of the area for Thursday through Sunday.  The LREF mean \nhigh temperatures also follow this pattern.  In contrast, the 12Z \nGFS extended MOS (MEX) high temperatures at KSTL for Thursday \nthrough Sunday are 95, 98, 98, and 94 respectively.  EC MOS is \nsimilar at 97, 96, 96, and 89.  Digging a little deeper in the LREF, \nthe max T probabilities are shifted to favor temperatures below 90 \ndegrees both Thursday and Friday.  The histogram is more even for \nthe weekend, but still slightly favors highs AOB 90 degrees.  With \nthis in mind, will continue to keep the cooler and wetter forecast \nfor the end of the week.",
